Notes on the transfer URI

Different wallets support different URI formats; EIP‑681 token transfer URIs are supported by some wallets but not all. If unsupported, you can paste the numeric amount into the wallet’s Send Tokens UI.
The HTML assumes 18 decimals if decimals are not provided in the payload. If you want decimals to be included, include decimals when encrypting (e.g., pass --decimals 18 to the Node script and include in payload).
Optional: small Express server to host encrypted envelope and return a short key-protected link If you want a server endpoint that serves the ciphertext and requires a short access token to retrieve the key, do NOT store keys in plaintext publicly. Instead use one of these patterns:
Host ciphertext publicly (data URL or file) and share the key out-of-band.
Host ciphertext behind an authenticated endpoint and give users auth to fetch key or ciphertext (e.g., signed pre-signed URL). Below is a minimal example that stores envelope files and returns them at /payload/:id while requiring a one-time passphrase to obtain the secret key from the server (you must implement secure storage).
I’m providing just an example pattern (not production-safe). Use HSMs or secret stores in production.

