That 404 is expected in two common cases — either the URL you opened contains the literal placeholder (YOUR_KEY) or you tried to open an RPC endpoint in a browser (Infura expects JSON‑RPC POSTs, not browser GETs). Here’s how to diagnose and fix it.

Quick checklist

Make sure you replaced YOUR_KEY with your actual Infura Project ID (or Project ID + secret via proper auth).
Infura endpoints are JSON‑RPC endpoints — they expect POST requests with a JSON body. Opening them in a browser with GET often returns “Page Not Found”.
Use the correct endpoint for the network (mainnet vs sepolia/goerli) and correct project credentials.
Don’t paste secrets here. If you want me to check a non‑secret URL, paste it (without key).
How to test your Infura endpoint correctly

Test with curl (JSON‑RPC POST) Replace <PROJECT_ID> with your Infura Project ID (do not include your project secret in the URL).
curl -i -X POST
-H "Content-Type: application/json"
--data '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"eth_blockNumber","params":[]}'
https://mainnet.infura.io/v3/<PROJECT_ID>

A working endpoint should return a JSON response with "result" containing the latest block number. If you get 404, check your PROJECT_ID and project settings.

Test with ethers.js (preferred) Node example using InfuraProvider (no raw URL required):
const { ethers } = require("ethers"); const provider = new ethers.providers.InfuraProvider("mainnet", { projectId: process.env.INFURA_PROJECT_ID, projectSecret: process.env.INFURA_PROJECT_SECRET // optional }); (async () => { const block = await provider.getBlockNumber(); console.log("block:", block); })();

